# User rules and verdict testing — API.md §Rule lists & rules
#
# Inline personal rules are a separate source from the downloaded lists
# (RULE_ENGINE.md §Sources) and are stored in /data, not in the config file.

@base = {{host}}/api/v1
@auth = Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Current inline rules, one per line in a JSON array.
GET {{base}}/rules/user
{{auth}}

### Replace the whole set — this is a PUT, not an append. Validated, then
### atomically swapped.
PUT {{base}}/rules/user
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rules": [
    "||tracker.example.com^",
    "@@||goodsite.example.com^",
    "! comments and blank lines are fine",
    "0.0.0.0 ads.example.net"
  ]
}

### Invalid syntax — expect 422 with PER-LINE messages naming the line number
### and quoting the offending text.
###
### Unlike a downloaded list, where unparseable lines are skipped and counted
### so a bad upstream cannot drop protection, hand-typed rules reject the whole
### request: silently dropping one would leave you believing you are protected.
PUT {{base}}/rules/user
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rules": [
    "||ads.example.com^",
    "||^",
    "@@||good.example.com^"
  ]
}

### DNS-inactive rules are still ACCEPTED (ADR-0003) — cosmetic and URL-pattern
### rules parse fine, they just do not participate in DNS matching. Rejecting
### them would break pasting a real-world list.
PUT {{base}}/rules/user
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rules": [
    "||ads.example.com^",
    "example.com##.ad-banner",
    "/ads/banner.gif"
  ]
}

### Clear all inline rules.
PUT {{base}}/rules/user
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "rules": []
}

### Dry-run a verdict without making a query. Answers which rule decided and
### which list it came from — the fastest way to explain "why is this blocked".
POST {{base}}/rules/test
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": "doubleclick.net",
  "qtype": "A"
}

### Expect verdict "pass" — no rule matched.
POST {{base}}/rules/test
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": "example.com",
  "qtype": "A"
}

### Subdomain matching: a rule on the parent covers this.
POST {{base}}/rules/test
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": "ads.doubleclick.net",
  "qtype": "AAAA"
}

### Per-client evaluation.
POST {{base}}/rules/test
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": "doubleclick.net",
  "qtype": "A",
  "client": "192.168.10.15"
}

### Empty domain — expect 422.
POST {{base}}/rules/test
{{auth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "domain": ""
}
